BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:IU Hamilton Lugar School
DTSTART:20261203T210000Z
DTEND:20261203T220000Z
LOCATION:Hamilton Lugar School
SUMMARY:CLACS Faculty Research Symposium with Luciana Namorato
DESCRIPTION:The Center for Latin American and Caribbean Studies (CLACS) w
 elcomes Dr. Luciana Namorato for The Other Fridas: Hidden Voices\, New Hi
 stories &amp\; Future Agendas of Latin American Women Artists\, part of t
 he Fall 2026 CLACS Faculty Research Symposium.Thursday\, December 3rd\, 4
 -5PM at the Hamilton Lugar School (355 N Eagleson Ave.) GA1060  This pres
 entation explores the recognition and representation of Latin American wo
 men artists\, considering how their voices and experiences can expand our
  understanding of the region's artistic histories.Bio:Luciana Namorato is
  Associate Professor of Portuguese and Luso-Brazilian Literatures at Indi
 ana University Bloomington. She holds an M.A. and a Ph.D. from the Univer
 sity of North Carolina at Chapel Hill. Her research focuses on Luso-Brazi
 lian and Latin American literatures\, with particular attention to the re
 lationships among literature\, gender\, and ethnic and national identitie
 s. As a Fulbright fellow\, she conducted postdoctoral research at the Uni
 versity of Coimbra. She is co-editor of The Other Fridas: The Lives and W
 orks of Latin American Women Artists (Lexington Books\, 2025)\, among oth
 er publications.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p data-pm-slice="1 1 []">  The Center for L
 atin American and Caribbean Studies (CLACS) welcomes Dr. <strong>Luciana 
 Namorato</strong> for <strong><em>The Other Fridas: Hidden Voices\, New H
 istories &amp\; Future Agendas of Latin American Women Artists</em></stro
 ng>\, part of the Fall 2026 CLACS Faculty Research Symposium.</p><p data-
 pm-slice="1 1 []"><strong>Thursday\, December 3rd</strong>\, <strong>4-5P
 M</strong> at the <strong>Hamilton Lugar School</strong> (355 N Eagleson 
 Ave.) <strong>GA1060</strong></p><p>  This presentation explores the reco
 gnition and representation of Latin American women artists\, considering 
 how their voices and experiences can expand our understanding of the regi
 on's artistic histories.</p><p><strong>Bio:</strong></p><p><strong>Lucian
 a Namorato</strong> is Associate Professor of Portuguese and Luso-Brazili
 an Literatures at Indiana University Bloomington. She holds an M.A. and a
  Ph.D. from the University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill. Her research
  focuses on Luso-Brazilian and Latin American literatures\, with particul
 ar attention to the relationships among literature\, gender\, and ethnic 
 and national identities. As a Fulbright fellow\, she conducted postdoctor
 al research at the University of Coimbra. She is co-editor of The Other F
 ridas: The Lives and Works of Latin American Women Artists (Lexington Boo
 ks\, 2025)\, among other publications.</p><p><picture class="lw_image lw_
 image32516  lw_align_left"><source media="(max-width: 500px)" type="image
